+# Graphify source-update CLI contract
+
+Status: tested local upstream patch
+Upstream package baseline: graphifyy 0.9.45; Graphify-Labs/graphify.
+
+## Public invocation
+
+- `graphify source-inventory --graph-dir DIR --output INVENTORY.json` reads only the public graph and writes a source inventory.
+- `graphify update-sources --graph-dir DIR --request REQUEST.json --output RESULT.json` extracts changed complete sources and publishes successful replacements.
+- `graphify update-sources ... --extraction EXTRACTION.json` consumes an already-produced public Graphify extraction artifact; useful for offline delivery/UAT and extraction reuse, never a dogpdkb internal call.
+
+## Request
+
+Schema: `graphify-source-update-1.0`. Top-level `sources` is a list. Each item contains caller `source_id`, `source_file` (desired public provenance filename), `path` (complete Markdown input), `replace` (list of previous public source_file names; defaults to source_file), optional `metadata` (public provenance fields), and `action` (`compile` default; `metadata` requires no extraction). Paths resolve against request directory. No deletions occur merely because a source is absent from the request.
+
+## Result
+
+Schema: `graphify-source-result-1.0`; `status` is completed/partial/failed, `graph` is the published graph path, `sources` contains source_id/source_file/action/status/error, and `counts` contains total/completed/failed. Report is written even for documented source failures. Exit 0 completed or no work, 2 partial with successful sources published, 1 request/publication failure. Individual extraction failure preserves that source old graph contribution. Caller versions remain opaque metadata, not a tool-side business scheduling policy.
+
+Inventory schema: `graphify-source-inventory-1.0`; `sources` contains each public source_file plus available source_id and metadata. This requires no semantic extraction.
+
+## Boundary
+
+Graphify owns extraction, cache, record identity, graph merging, complete-source replacement and atomic graph publication. dogpdkb only invokes this CLI and consumes its documented report/inventory. This document is a target contract until upstream implementation/tests and install identity below are complete.
+
+## Extraction artifact
+
+`--extraction` accepts schema `graphify-source-extraction-1.0` with `sources` rows:
+`{"source_id":"a","status":"completed","extraction":{"nodes":[],"edges":[],"hyperedges":[]}}`.
+Failed rows use `status: failed` and `error`. Omitted rows fail individually. This
+public seam never contacts a provider. Duplicate same-ID chunk nodes are accepted
+and deduplicated by Graphify. Empty completed extractions remove the old source.
+
+Without this flag, complete Markdown inputs are extracted one source at a time
+with Graphify's own content cache and extraction pipeline. `--backend`, `--model`,
+and `--max-concurrency` configure that pipeline; existing provider environment
+variables apply. Missing files, failed/truncated chunks and uncovered inputs fail
+the source and preserve its old contribution. Successful sources publish together.
+Exit 2 also applies when every source failed (nothing is published).
+
+Metadata actions need neither input files nor extraction results. They update
+provenance fields while retaining graph record identities. Structural fields such
+as id/label/source/target cannot be metadata. Overlapping replacement requests are
+rejected. This command does not extract unrequested historical documents or run
+clustering/paid labeling; existing unrelated graph attributes are retained.
+
+Offline verification: `python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_source_update_cli.py -v`.
+Tests run the public subprocess CLI, including an ephemeral localhost provider;
+no model service, credentials, corpus, or production graph is used.
